{%- comment -%}
  snippets/lada-product-card.liquid

  A FORK of snippets/product-card.liquid, not a restyle of it. The shared
  .pcard sells other people's cloth under Khan Jee's name, so it carries
  a white-label vendor rule, a KJC SKU fallback, a compare-at
  strikethrough, a percent-off chip, a Khan Jee Seal badge and a
  pre-order chip. Lada is the house's own label: no supplier to hide,
  nothing discounted, and a seal badge on a Lada card would be the house
  stamping its own name on its own garment. Overriding six rules off a
  shared snippet is how that snippet ends up owned by two pages and safe
  to edit for neither, so this stays a separate file.

  WHY THIS FILE WAS CUT DOWN, AND WHAT CAME OFF.
  The previous version of this card carried six things. The client
  rejected the whole Lada build for the same reason each time: too much
  writing, too many options, and words no customer would use. The
  reference he gave is faizasaqlain.pk, where a card is a photograph and
  a name and nothing else. So the following were deleted outright and
  must not come back:

    - the cloth-and-cut eyebrow (lada.cloth / lada.cut / custom.by_fabric).
      It was there to teach the filter vocabulary while you browsed.
      Everything in Lada is cotton, so it taught one word, and that word
      was on every card.
    - the made-to-measure price substitution and its "Measured in the
      shop" link. Alamgeer is cut to measurement as a matter of course,
      so the exception was the rule, and the price slot is for a price.
      The client has confirmed prices are shown and products add to the bag.
    - every badge, chip and seal
    - the SKU
    - the compare-at price and the percent-off figure
    - any swatch. Colour is chosen in the quick view panel or on the
      product page, not on a card in a grid.

  WHAT IS LEFT, AND IT IS DELIBERATELY ONLY THIS:
    1. the primary photograph at 4:5, same ratio as .pcard__media site-wide
    2. the piece name, 15px/500, sentence case
    3. the price, 15px/500, tabular, same size as the name and never
       larger, never coloured

  Plus two controls, both of which live over the bottom of the plate on
  a pointer device and in the card's own flow on touch. They are not a
  fourth and fifth thing on the card: at rest on a desktop the card is
  the photograph, the name and the price, and nothing else is painted.

  WHAT THIS CARD NOW SHARES WITH .pcard, AND WHY THAT MATTERS MORE THAN
  THE FORK DID.

  The client's complaint about Lada was that it reads as a different
  website. A fork is the right call for the register and the wrong call
  for the grammar, so everything that is not the register has been put
  back in step with snippets/product-card.liquid:

    - the title rule. Both cards now print
      `product.title | split: ' - ' | first`. Shopify titles on this
      store carry a " - suffix" the shop uses for its own sorting, and
      one card trimming it while the other did not meant the SAME piece
      read with two different names depending on which grid you found
      it in. An earlier note here argued the suffix was part of a Lada
      piece's proper name; it is not, it is the shop's filing, and
      hiding it in one grid and not the other was the visible symptom.
    - the touch swap. See data-pcard on the article below.
    - element order within each block, the .vh accessible-name idiom on
      the controls, and the data-kj-atc / data-kj-atc-error contract.
    - `card_sizes`, the optional slot string, same name and same meaning
      on both cards.

  One thing the shared card grew that this one does not need: its .vh
  status line. Its badges (sold out, percent off, the seal, pre order)
  all sit inside the aria-hidden photograph anchor, so they had to be
  repeated as text for a screen reader. This card has no badges at all,
  and the one state it does show, sold out, is already plain text in the
  price line and already in the reading order.

  What stays forked is only what the register needs: no seal badge, no
  compare-at, no percent-off chip, no SKU eyebrow, and the controls last
  in the DOM rather than wrapped in a .pcard__top. Each of those is
  argued at the point it happens.

  Usage:
    {% render 'lada-product-card', product: product, index: forloop.index0,
       quickview_label: quickview_label, add_label: add_label,
       sold_label: sold_label %}

  Every label is a string the client owns in the calling section's
  schema, so nothing customer-visible is hard-coded here. The defaults
  below are only the fallback for a caller that forgets to pass one, and
  they are sentence case on purpose: uppercase on this card comes from
  text-transform in CSS and is never typed into a setting value.

  Callers may still pass mtm_tag, mtm_label, mtm_url, options_label and
  divider. They are ignored rather than rejected, so sections/lada-collection
  .liquid keeps rendering while it is being cut down in its own pass.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign quickview_label = quickview_label | default: 'Quick view'
  assign add_label       = add_label       | default: 'Add to bag'
  assign sold_label      = sold_label      | default: 'Sold out'

  # The hover pair. Frame 2 is a second model frame from the same set,
  # never a detail shot. If a product has only one image there is no
  # pair and .lcard--pair is never added, so the hover rules do not
  # exist for that card at all. That is the whole no-phantom-state fix:
  # not a hover that cross-fades a photograph into itself, no hover.
  assign alt_media = null
  if product.media.size > 1
    assign alt_media = product.media[1]
  endif

  # Every frame in this grid is lazy, including the first row, and
  # `index` is accepted only so a caller has something to pass. The
  # shared .pcard eager-loads its first four because it sits on a
  # template where the grid IS the top of the page. This grid never is:
  # on the gateway it sits behind a full-plate hero, and on the two
  # listings it sits behind the page heading and the filter rail. Eager
  # loading four 4:5 garment frames there does not make them arrive
  # sooner, it makes them compete with the element that is actually the
  # LCP.
  assign loading = 'lazy'

  # Never ask Shopify for a width the source file does not have. An
  # upscale request returns the native file anyway but writes a
  # candidate the browser may pick on a 3x screen and then render soft.
  # The native width is always the last candidate and the smaller steps
  # are only added when the source actually clears them.
  assign main_w = product.featured_media.width | default: 900
  assign main_src_w = 600
  if main_w < main_src_w
    assign main_src_w = main_w
  endif
  assign alt_w = alt_media.width | default: 900
  assign alt_src_w = 600
  if alt_w < alt_src_w
    assign alt_src_w = alt_w
  endif

  # SAME RULE AS THE SHARED CARD, deliberately. See the header.
  # `split` on a title with no " - " in it returns the whole title as the
  # only element, so a clean name passes through untouched.
  assign card_title = product.title | split: ' - ' | first

  # The stagger for the site's scroll reveal. index is the caller's
  # forloop.index0 and is defaulted here because the snippet is also
  # rendered by callers that have no loop to count.
  #
  # Capped at the seventh card, which is 420ms. Past that the last card
  # in a long grid is still waiting to start after the visitor has
  # scrolled it back off screen, which reads as a stuck page rather than
  # as an entrance. Same cap, for the same reason, as the Lada observer
  # in kj-v2.js.
  assign reveal_i = index | default: 0
  assign reveal_d = reveal_i | times: 60 | at_most: 420

  # A price slot is only rendered when it has something true to say. A
  # product sitting at 0.00 because a variant was never priced must not
  # print a currency symbol against nothing, and inventing a phrase to
  # fill the gap is the writing this card was cut down to remove.
  assign has_price = false
  if product.price > 0
    assign has_price = true
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  The slot, named and defaulted exactly as snippets/product-card.liquid
  names and defaults it, so a caller hands either card its own `sizes`
  string the same way. The default below is a .pgrid cell: 283px is the
  cell at the 1180px wrap cap, and the vw steps are the three column
  counts under it.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign slot = card_sizes | default: '(min-width:1240px) 283px, (min-width:1100px) 23vw, (min-width:760px) 31vw, 46vw' -%}

{%- comment -%}
  data-pcard IS THE TOUCH HOOK, NOT A STYLE.

  On a pointer device the second photograph arrives on :hover, which
  CSS does on its own. A phone has no hover, so kj-v2.js gives the
  shared card a first-tap-swaps / second-tap-navigates fallback, and
  the Lada card had none: on the device most of this shop is browsed
  on, the second frame simply never existed.

  That script finds a card by [data-pcard] and then looks inside it for
  .pcard__img--alt and .pcard__link. Those two are carried alongside the
  .lcard names rather than instead of them, so the existing fallback
  binds with no change to the JavaScript at all. Neither borrowed class
  drags any white-register paint over: .pcard__img--alt is opacity:0,
  which .lcard__img--alt already is, and .pcard__link::after is the same
  inset:0 overlay as .lcard__link::after. Every visible rule on this card
  still comes from .lcard.

  data-pcard is only emitted when there is a second frame to swap to.
  Without that gate the script would add a click handler that swallows
  the first tap on a card with nothing to show for it, turning a working
  link into a dead one.

  THE ONE THING THIS NEEDS FROM THE STYLESHEET. kj-v2.js paints the
  swapped state by putting .is-touched on the card, and the rule that
  reads it is written against the white card:

      .pcard.is-touched .pcard__img--alt{opacity:1}

  which cannot match an .lcard article. The dark twin of that rule,
  .lcard.is-touched .lcard__img--alt{opacity:1}, belongs beside the
  existing .lcard--pair hover rules in kj-v2.css. Until it lands the
  first tap is swallowed and shows nothing, so if this hook is ever
  reverted, revert it together with that rule and not on its own.

  data-reveal is the site's own scroll reveal, the hook
  sections/category-grid.liquid already uses, so the Lada wall of
  photographs arrives the same way the white shop's category grid does.
{%- endcomment -%}
<article class="lcard{% if alt_media %} lcard--pair{% endif %}"
         {% if alt_media %}data-pcard{% endif %}
         data-reveal style="--d:{{ reveal_d }}ms">

  {%- comment -%}
    The image link is marked decorative so the name below stays the one
    announced link. A second real link to the same product would make
    every card announce itself twice and double the tab stops across a
    twelve-card grid.

    Its ::after covers the PHOTOGRAPH, not the whole card: this element
    is both .lcard__media and .lcard__link, and .lcard__media is the
    position:relative box, so inset:0 resolves to the plate. The two
    controls sit above it on z-index and stay clickable.

    With no picked image the box is simply left empty. .lcard__media
    holds the 4:5 ratio and paints --lada-panel, so an unshot piece
    reads as a reserved plate rather than a broken img or a collapsed
    row. The client loads every photograph himself after handover, so
    this is the state the whole grid ships in and it has to look
    intentional rather than broken.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <a class="lcard__media lcard__link pcard__link" href="{{ product.url }}" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
    {%- if product.featured_media -%}
      <img class="lcard__img lcard__img--main"
           src="{{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: main_src_w }}"
           srcset="{%- if main_w > 400 %}{{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 400 }} 400w, {% endif -%}
                   {%- if main_w > 600 %}{{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 600 }} 600w, {% endif -%}
                   {%- if main_w > 900 %}{{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 900 }} 900w, {% endif -%}
                   {{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: main_w }} {{ main_w }}w"
           sizes="{{ slot }}"
           width="{{ product.featured_media.width }}" height="{{ product.featured_media.height }}"
           loading="{{ loading }}" decoding="async"
           alt="{{ product.featured_media.alt | default: card_title | escape }}">
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if alt_media -%}
      <img class="lcard__img lcard__img--alt pcard__img--alt"
           src="{{ alt_media | image_url: width: alt_src_w }}"
           srcset="{%- if alt_w > 400 %}{{ alt_media | image_url: width: 400 }} 400w, {% endif -%}
                   {%- if alt_w > 600 %}{{ alt_media | image_url: width: 600 }} 600w, {% endif -%}
                   {%- if alt_w > 900 %}{{ alt_media | image_url: width: 900 }} 900w, {% endif -%}
                   {{ alt_media | image_url: width: alt_w }} {{ alt_w }}w"
           sizes="{{ slot }}"
           width="{{ alt_media.width }}" height="{{ alt_media.height }}"
           loading="lazy" decoding="async" aria-hidden="true" alt="">
    {%- endif -%}
  </a>

  <h3 class="lcard__title"><a href="{{ product.url }}">{{ card_title }}</a></h3>

  {%- comment -%}
    Price slot. Never a compare-at, never a percentage, never coloured,
    never larger than the name.

    product.price is the lowest variant price. Alamgeer is priced by the
    piece and Ready to Wear by the garment, not by the size, so in
    practice every variant of a Lada product carries the same figure and
    a "from" prefix would be a word added to every card to describe a
    case that does not occur. If the client ever prices S below L, the
    prefix is a one-line change here and a new setting, not a redesign.

    Sold out rides in this line as 11px caps rather than as a chip over
    the photograph, because a chip is the first thing that turns a wall
    of garments back into a stock table. It stays because the add to bag
    control disappears when a piece is unavailable, and a card that
    silently loses its only button reads as a bug.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if has_price or product.available == false -%}
    <p class="lcard__price">
      {%- if has_price -%}<span>{{ product.price | money }}</span>{%- endif -%}
      {%- unless product.available -%}<span class="lcard__state">{{ sold_label }}</span>{%- endunless -%}
    </p>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    The controls.

    LAST IN THE DOM ON PURPOSE. On a pointer device CSS moves this block
    into the photograph's own grid cell and bottom-aligns it, so the
    resting grid stays pure photography and the controls rise over the
    bottom of the plate on hover. Placing it by grid area rather than by
    position:absolute is what lets the name and the price stay ABOVE it
    in source order. The shared .pcard had to wrap its image in a
    .pcard__top so `bottom` had something to resolve against, and that
    wrapper is exactly what forces its button in front of the name and
    the price for a screen reader. Same overlay, honest reading order.

    On anything without hover the same markup is just the last item in
    the card's flex column: permanent 44px targets, in flow, never a
    floating bar and never a black strip parked across every tablet
    photograph.

    QUICK VIEW. A real <button>, not a link, because it opens a panel on
    this page rather than going anywhere. Another agent owns that panel
    and its JS; this file only emits the trigger and the two attributes
    the panel reads, data-lada-quickview and data-product-url.

    Its accessible name is built from visible text plus a visually
    hidden piece title, not from an aria-label. Two reasons. A grid of
    twelve buttons all named "Quick view" is unusable with a screen
    reader's element list, and an aria-label would replace the visible
    words, which breaks voice control for anyone who says "click quick
    view". Concatenating gives "Quick view, Zafraan" while the button
    still matches on the words a person can see.

    With JavaScript off the trigger does nothing, which is the same
    bargain Shopify's own quick add makes in the base theme. It costs
    nothing here because the photograph and the name are both real links
    to the product page, so the card is never a dead end.

    ADD TO BAG. Unchanged contract: assets/kj-cart.js listens for submit
    on form[data-kj-atc], posts to /cart/add.js, and writes any failure
    into [data-kj-atc-error] inside that form. Do not invent a new hook
    here. Without JS it is still a plain form post to /cart/add and still
    buys the piece.

    The form only renders for a genuinely single-variant piece, which on
    Lada is the exception: Alamgeer carries colour options and Ready to
    Wear carries S, M and L. Everything else gets the quick view trigger
    alone. The old "Choose options" link is gone because that is now the
    quick view's entire job, and two controls a centimetre apart sending
    a customer to the same place is the overwhelm the client rejected.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="lcard__act">
    <button class="lcard__quick lcard__quick--view" type="button"
            data-lada-quickview
            data-product-url="{{ product.url }}">
      <span class="lcard__quick-t">{{ quickview_label }}</span>
      <span class="vh">, {{ card_title }}</span>
    </button>

    {%- if product.available and product.variants.size == 1 -%}
      <form class="lcard__add" action="{{ routes.cart_add_url }}" method="post"
            enctype="multipart/form-data" data-kj-atc>
        <input type="hidden" name="id" value="{{ product.selected_or_first_available_variant.id }}">
        <input type="hidden" name="quantity" value="1">
        <button type="submit" class="lcard__quick lcard__quick--add">
          <span class="lcard__quick-t">{{ add_label }}</span>
          <span class="vh">, {{ card_title }}</span>
          <span class="lcard__spin" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </button>
        <p class="lcard__err" data-kj-atc-error role="alert" hidden></p>
      </form>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>

</article>
